{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HtamZ7NxfSu7CwvrRJ3Q13","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/quic.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `quic.h` is the kitchen-sink public header for the\n4\t//! quic-core library: it defines the protocol error codes, the\n5\t//! transport-parameter identifiers, the application-facing enums, the\n6\t//! opaque QUIC / connection / path types, the public structs that\n7\t//! cross the API boundary (`TransportParameters`, `PathQuality`,\n8\t//! `PerAckState`, `CongestionAlgorithm`, …)\n9\t//! and the dozens of free functions that make up the application API.\n10\t//!\n11\t//! Phase 4: all function bodies have been filled in.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes that apply throughout\n14\t//! this module:\n15\t//!\n16\t//! * `Quic`, `Connection`, `Path` are\n17\t//!   opaque types defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares\n18\t//!   them as empty structs here so the public API can refer to them;\n19\t//!   the real layout lands when the internal header is translated.\n20\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields map to\n21\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`].  The C source uses tagged unions of\n22\t//!   `sockaddr_in` / `sockaddr_in6`, which `SocketAddr` models\n23\t//!   directly.  Output parameters of type `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n24\t//!   become a `SocketAddr` returned by value.\n25\t//! * Function-pointer typedefs become traits (one trait per typedef\n26\t//!   by default).  Trait grouping is used for the four-function\n27\t//!   `CongestionAlgorithm` vtable, where the C side\n28\t//!   always installs the four together.\n29\t//! * `void*` \"callback context\" arguments are folded into the trait\n30\t//!   implementor's state.  Per-stream / per-path application\n31\t//!   contexts that flow through the stack (e.g. `stream_ctx`,\n32\t//!   `app_stream_ctx`) become `Option<Box<dyn core::any::Any>>` —\n33\t//!   the application produces them, downcasts on retrieval.\n34\t//! * C `int` / `int64_t` return values that encode 0/-1 or 0/error\n35\t//!   status become `Result<T, Error>`.  `i32` / `i64` are kept where\n36\t//!   the C value is a real integer (e.g., wake delays in microseconds,\n37\t//!   interface index).\n38\t//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` bitfields collapse to `bool`.\n39\t//! * `va_list` has no Rust counterpart; the `_v` log helpers are\n40\t//!   omitted in favour of one entry point that takes\n41\t//!   `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`.\n42\t//! * `extern` global state (`congestion_control_algorithms`)\n43\t//!   becomes accessor functions that hand out a borrowed slice; the\n44\t//!   companion `_nb_` length variable disappears (the slice carries\n45\t//!   its length).\n46\t\n47\t// Many translated functions mirror C signatures with >7 parameters.\n48\t// Builder patterns or signature reshaping were not part of Phase 1.\n49\t#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n50\t\n51\tpub mod arena;\n52\tpub mod bbr;\n53\tpub mod bbr1;\n54\tpub mod binlog;\n55\tpub mod bytestream;\n56\tpub mod c4;\n57\tpub mod cc_common;\n58\tpub mod config;\n59\tpub mod crypto;\n60\tpub mod cubic;\n61\tpub mod ech;\n62\tpub mod errors;\n63\tpub mod fastcc;\n64\tpub mod frames;\n65\tpub mod hash;\n66\tpub mod header_protection;\n67\tpub mod internal;\n68\tpub mod lb;\n69\tpub mod logger;\n70\tpub mod newreno;\n71\tpub mod packet_loop;\n72\tpub mod performance_log;\n73\tpub mod prague;\n74\tpub mod qlog;\n75\tpub mod siphash;\n76\tpub mod socks;\n77\tpub mod socks_socket2;\n78\tpub mod spinbit;\n79\tpub mod splay;\n80\tpub mod stream;\n81\tpub mod sys;\n82\t#[cfg(test)]\n83\tpub mod tests;\n84\tpub mod textlog;\n85\tpub mod tls;\n86\tpub mod tls_api;\n87\tpub mod tp;\n88\tpub mod utils;\n89\t\n90\t// Re-export the TP shapes from the public crate root for backwards\n91\t// compatibility with callers that already wrote `crate::TransportParameters`.\n92\tpub use tp::{PreferredAddress, TransportParameter, TransportParameters, VersionNegotiation};\n93\t\n94\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n95\t\n96\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n97\t// Version string and error class.\n98\t//\n99\t// Error values come straight from `#define` and would naturally be\n100\t// untyped in C; in Rust we commit to `u64` because the application\n101\t// API takes them as `uint64_t error_code` parameters and returns\n102\t// them in `get_local_error` etc.\n103\t\n104\t/// Library version string.  C: `VERSION`.\n105\tpub const VERSION: &str = \"1.1.48.0\";\n106\t\n107\t/// Crate-level error type for fallible operations.\n108\t///\n109\t/// Phase 4 implementations return specific variants based on the C\n110\t/// control flow.  The initial variant set covers the broad failure\n111\t/// shapes; additional variants may be added as needed.  Marked\n112\t/// `#[non_exhaustive]` so adding\n113\t/// variants later isn't a breaking change.\n114\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n115\t#[non_exhaustive]\n116\tpub enum Error {\n117\t    /// Catch-all for C paths that collapse to a generic failure.\n118\t    Generic,\n119\t    /// Allocation failed.\n120\t    Memory,\n121\t    /// Caller supplied an invalid argument.\n122\t    InvalidArgument,\n123\t    /// A buffer the caller supplied was too small.\n124\t    BufferTooSmall,\n125\t    /// Caller-supplied file does not exist.\n126\t    NoSuchFile,\n127\t    /// Caller-supplied file was malformed.\n128\t    InvalidFile,\n129\t    /// Connection has been disconnected.\n130\t    Disconnected,\n131\t    /// Connection state didn't permit the requested operation.\n132\t    InvalidState,\n133\t    /// Operation produced or received a malformed QUIC frame.\n134\t    InvalidFrame,\n135\t    /// TLS / crypto error.\n136\t    Tls,\n137\t    /// Other QUIC protocol error; the wrapped value is the\n138\t    /// numeric `ERROR_*` code defined in this module.\n139\t    Protocol(u64),\n140\t}\n141\t\n142\timpl core::fmt::Display for Error {\n143\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n144\t        write!(f, \"{:?}\", self)\n145\t    }\n146\t}\n147\t\n148\timpl core::error::Error for Error {}\n149\t\n150\t/// Crate-wide `Result` alias defaulting the error type to [`Error`].\n151\tpub type Result<T, E = Error> = core::result::Result<T, E>;\n152\t\n153\t/// Wall-clock instant in microseconds since some application-defined\n154\t/// epoch.  Replaces the C `uint64_t current_time` parameter that\n155\t/// threads through every API call needing \"now\".  The type is\n156\t/// `Copy`, supports arithmetic with [`Duration`], and converts to\n157\t/// `u64` cheaply via `.ticks()` when crossing wire boundaries.\n158\t///\n159\t/// Phase 2 decision: per-call `current_time: Instant` parameters\n160\t/// stay (matches the C source's design — caller decides what time\n161\t/// it is).  No `Clock` trait at the library level; applications\n162\t/// can build a clock-injecting wrapper around the calls if they\n163\t/// want that style.\n164\tpub type Instant = fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n165\t\n166\t/// Duration in microseconds.  Companion to [`Instant`].  Use for\n167\t/// timeouts, RTTs, jitter, etc.  In the C source these are bare\n168\t/// `uint64_t` values labelled by context (`idle_timeout`,\n169\t/// `microsec_latency`, …); the typed alias makes the unit explicit.\n170\tpub type Duration = fugit::Duration<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n171\t\n172\t// Error codes (picoquic-internal `ERROR_*` and protocol-defined\n173\t// `TRANSPORT_*` / TLS-alert) live as enums in [`crate::errors`].\n174\tpub use errors::{InternalError, TransportError};\n175\t\n176\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n177\t// Packet sizes and miscellaneous constants.\n178\t\n179\tpub const MAX_PACKET_SIZE: usize = 1536;\n180\tpub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV4: usize = 1252;\n181\tpub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV6: usize = 1232;\n182\tpub const RESET_SECRET_SIZE: usize = 16;\n183\tpub const RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE: usize = 23;\n184\tpub const RESET_PACKET_MIN_SIZE: usize = RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE + RESET_SECRET_SIZE;\n185\tpub const MAX_CRYPTO_BUFFER_GAP: usize = 16384;\n186\t\n187\tpub const LOG_PACKET_MAX_SEQUENCE: u32 = 100;\n188\t\n189\t/// First 4 bytes of `SHA256(\"QUIC Masque\")`.  Used as a sentinel\n190\t/// interface index where no real OS interface is meaningful.\n191\tpub const RESERVED_IF_INDEX: u32 = 0x09cb8ed3;\n192\t\n193\t// TLS cipher-suite identifiers (IANA).\n194\tpub const AES_128_GCM_SHA256: u16 = 0x1301;\n195\tpub const AES_256_GCM_SHA384: u16 = 0x1302;\n196\tpub const CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256: u16 = 0x1303;\n197\t\n198\tpub const GROUP_SECP256R1: u16 = 23;\n199\t\n200\t// ECN code points for the IP header `tos` byte.\n201\tpub const ECN_ECT_0: u8 = 0x02;\n202\tpub const ECN_ECT_1: u8 = 0x01;\n203\tpub const ECN_CE: u8 = 0x03;\n204\t\n205\t/// C macro `FOURCC(a, b, c, d)`.  Produces a 32-bit code from four\n206\t/// bytes in little-endian order.\n207\tpub const fn fourcc(a: u8, b: u8, c: u8, d: u8) -> u32 {\n208\t    u32::from_le_bytes([a, b, c, d])\n209\t}\n210\t\n211\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n212\t// Connection state.\n213\t\n214\t/// Connection-state machine, listing the QUIC connection states a\n215\t/// `Connection` walks through from initial handshake to teardown.\n216\t/// Discriminants follow the declaration order of the C `state_enum`.\n217\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]\n218\tpub enum State {\n219\t    ClientInit,\n220\t    ClientInitSent,\n221\t    ClientRenegotiate,\n222\t    ClientRetryReceived,\n223\t    ClientInitResent,\n224\t    ServerInit,\n225\t    ServerHandshake,\n226\t    ClientHandshakeStart,\n227\t    HandshakeFailure,\n228\t    HandshakeFailureResend,\n229\t    ClientAlmostReady,\n230\t    ServerFalseStart,\n231\t    ServerAlmostReady,\n232\t    ClientReadyStart,\n233\t    Ready,\n234\t    Disconnecting,\n235\t    ClosingReceived,\n236\t    Closing,\n237\t    Draining,\n238\t    Disconnected,\n239\t}\n240\t\n241\t// `TransportParameter`, `PreferredAddress`, `VersionNegotiation`,\n242\t// `TransportParameters`, and `TransportParameter0RttKind` live in\n243\t// [`crate::tp`].\n244\t\n245\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n246\t// Packet contexts and enumerated policy types.\n247\t\n248\t/// Encryption-level packet context.  Each value selects one of the\n249\t/// three QUIC packet-number spaces (Initial, Handshake, 1-RTT).\n250\t/// The C enum's trailing `nb_packet_context` count is exposed as\n251\t/// the [`NB_PACKET_CONTEXT`] constant below.\n252\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n253\t#[repr(u8)]\n254\tpub enum PacketContext {\n255\t    Application = 0,\n256\t    Handshake = 1,\n257\t    Initial = 2,\n258\t}\n259\t\n260\t/// Number of packet contexts (matches the variant count of\n261\t/// [`PacketContext`]).\n262\tpub const NB_PACKET_CONTEXT: usize = 3;\n263\t\n264\t/// Path-MTU-discovery policy for a connection.\n265\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n266\t#[repr(u8)]\n267\tpub enum PmtudPolicy {\n268\t    /// Default opportunistic PMTUD.\n269\t    #[default]\n270\t    Basic = 0,\n271\t    /// Force PMTUD as soon as possible.\n272\t    Required = 1,\n273\t    /// Only do PMTUD if a lot of data has to be sent.\n274\t    Delayed = 2,\n275\t    /// Never do PMTUD.\n276\t    Blocked = 3,\n277\t}\n278\t\n279\t/// Spin-bit variant policy.\n280\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n281\t#[repr(u8)]\n282\tpub enum SpinbitVersion {\n283\t    /// Default behaviour, per the spin-bit draft.\n284\t    #[default]\n285\t    Basic = 0,\n286\t    /// Randomise per packet.\n287\t    Random = 1,\n288\t    /// Null behaviour, randomised per path.\n289\t    Null = 2,\n290\t    /// Test-only \"always on\" mode.  Not valid as a per-connection\n291\t    /// override (server only; see [`Quic::set_default_spinbit_policy`]).\n292\t    On = 3,\n293\t}\n294\t\n295\t/// Loss-bit support level.\n296\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n297\t#[repr(u8)]\n298\tpub enum LossbitVersion {\n299\t    /// Loss bits disabled.\n300\t    #[default]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MHtBgZYddCc3d77yZouPcF","session_id":"4f4cd1bb-ca1b-49c0-a612-160336c885bd","uuid":"54a34216-ee4d-4aa9-a35a-fca8472f7440","timestamp":"2026-05-07T13:55:07.930Z"}
